  {
    "episode_id": 77300,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77300.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "Well, I don't know what was said in the meeting that President Trump had with President Putin. That remains between those two leaders. But I tell you, Congress has been very unified on this point that the Russians did interfere in our election and the indictments that Mueller put out here just before the president met in Helsinki with Putin are clear. And we're not talking just about Russians buying Facebook posts or social media influences. We're talking about breaking into voter files. We're talking about breaking into the DNC servers. This ought to have a chilling effect on Americans who believe in protecting our democracy and the sacred right to vote and that every vote counts. So the evidence is overwhelming on this. And we confronted, we confronted Foreign Minister Lavrov on this point. In fact, I think he was almost flabbergasted by every one of us. We had seven senators and one member of the House. Every one of us, and we went around the horn here talking issues with Lavrov, talking issues with Chairman Volotin of the Duma, it was a consistent message, do not interfere in our elections. And the Russians are seeking to undermine democracy. They don't stand for democracy. They stand to undermine democracy. We must vigorously defend it. And I was grateful to see the present statement yesterday saying that the Russians did interfere in our elections. He agrees with the intelligence community. That's where I'm at. That's where all seven senators plus the member of the House were when we sent that message to the Russians in Moscow a couple of weeks ago. And that's where we need to be going forward.",
