  {
    "episode_id": 77300,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77300.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "Well, his statement yesterday is solid. And I, when I look at this relationship between the United States and Russia, I think it's important to understand why is this relationship important? Why was it important for President Trump to meet with President Putin in Helsinki? Ambassador Huntsman, John Huntsman, who was a US ambassador to Russia based in Moscow. He previously served as ambassador in Singapore, ambassador in China. I have tremendous respect from Ambassador Huntsman. In fact, I told President Trump, after our visit to Russia, we were riding on the plane together out to Montana to you have the right man at the right time and the right job with Huntsman being in Moscow. This is how Ambassador Huntsman framed it. When we sat down before we had our meetings with the Russian leadership, Ambassador Huntsman made this point. I'm quoting him as best as I remember what he said. He said this. He said the U.S.-China relationship. to fail because it's the two world's largest economies. You know, I worked in China for five and a half years at Procter & Gamble. I lead a congressional delegation in China every year because of the importance of the fact they are a $12 trillion economy, the U.S. is about a $19 trillion economy, where the two world's largest economies buy a long shot. That's why that relationship is too big to fail. Then he pivoted over and told us he said the U.S.-Russia relationship is too big to fail because these two nations control 90% of the nuclear stockpile in the world, the nuclear weapons. Over 14,000 nuclear weapons are controlled by between the United States and Russia. So I would never want to set a high expectation in terms of an outcome from a summit between Russia and the United States, between two presidents. between President Putin and President Trump. But I will say this, the dialogue is very important to keep the phone channels open because our two nations represent 90% of the nuclear stockpile. I think Ambassador Huntsman framed that well. That's why I applaud the President in engaging Russia. I applaud Chairman Dunford of the Joint Chief of Staff in engaging Russian military to be conflict in Syria. And that's why I thought it was important that we had a U.S. delegation of members of the House and Senate in Moscow the first time in three years. When you have two superpowers that relates to nuclear stockpiles, radio silence is not a good thing. Keeping communication channels open is a better alternative.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
