  {
    "episode_id": 77300,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77300.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, listen, we have three branches of government for a very important reason. There's an executive branch that allows the president to have discussions with foreign leaders. It's not uncommon for the president to pick up the phone and call a foreign leader, whether it be President Putin or it would be Abe in Japan or President Xi. And so the president has a prerogative to have conversations with foreign leaders. as he or she sees fit. And so I think it's important for us to recognize that President Trump, number one, has opened up a dialogue with President Putin to try to improve a relationship that Ambassador Huntsman said is probably the lowest point post-Cold War the U.S. Russia relationship has ever been. uh... that is very concerning considering the existential threat that the nuclear stockpiles the nations uh... present to the rest of the world and so that dialogue i think is a good thing start a dialogue uh... in the present was clear yesterday in his statement that uh... he supports the conclusion from our intelligence community from your great americans from jina has built a damn coat to previously Mike Pompeo and others. And so I take the present at what he said and I think we need to move forward.",
