  {
    "episode_id": 77300,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77300.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "Well, regarding what needs to happen next, I think the Congress needs to continue to take a look at whether it's funding mechanisms, additional policies to prevent foreign interference in US elections. I think this needs to stay as an important issue for Congress because I'm not convinced the Russians are finished. I'm not convinced there could be other bad actors around the world that may seek to do something similar to undermine democracy. Because this is what we're dealing with. We're dealing with a nation, the United States, that stands for freedom, democracy, has delegated the powers to the people against tyrannies. And authoritarian regimes, they stand against what we stand for. And that, you know, what's core to our democracy is the ability for people to vote and that we have confidence in the outcomes of elections. So I think this needs to remain an important issue for Congress to address whether it's additional funding on cyber protections and hardening some of these targets of bad actors. I'll tell you, last night I was presiding over the Senate It was from 6.38 p.m. last evening in Washington D.C. and I thought Marco Rubio gave a very, very thoughtful speech. There was nobody in the chamber. Marco and me and probably C-Span. Marco really talked about this needs to not become a partisan Republican versus Democrat issue. This really is when we need to come together on here in terms of protecting the democratic process.",
