  {
    "episode_id": 77300,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77300.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 61,
    "turn_text": "Well, listen, I think, as I stated earlier about how Ambassador Huntsman framed their relationship and why this is so important. You know, Russia economically is not a superpower. They're usually about 10th or 11th in terms of GDP globally. So economically, they're not a superpower. Militarily, they are, and that's because of nuclear weapons. So I think with the chatter out here, the media and the press love to talk about this, that and different conspiracy theories. I'll tell you what, we need to have a better relationship between Russia and the United States. because of the nuclear stockpiles. If nothing else, it starts there. I think there's some room for some small winds in terms of ensuring that Russia complies with INF. The nuclear trees are in place here for short, medium-range nuclear weapons. So I think the relationship is important. I applaud the president for taking the risk to engage Putin. As you know, in the history of the summits between past presidents and Russian leaders, it oftentimes hasn't ended well. And President Trump took that risk. I think it's a risk worth taking. And I think it's important here because, again, of the strategic threat that Russia presents not just the United States but to the world.",
