    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/7/77300.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 68,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and I think it highlights the broader issue that a lot of thinking Republicans, smart Republicans, elected, elected leader Republicans have around all of this is that you just have these glaring inconsistencies that President Trump himself presents. There are times where what President Trump says is anathema to not just the Republican Party, but all of American presidential traditions. And it's also that unique style, and you think, I think Senator Danes hinted at this, that the president's the negotiator, and he thinks he gets himself on the room and can negotiate, and so it's opening up these conversations. But man, is it awkward and uncomfortable to a generation of elected leaders who have spent their lives respecting intelligence institutions, realizing that someone like Vladimir Putin is a manipulative person who is going to use even these personal interactions to his benefit, And looking on a world stage like you're cow-towing to the Russians. There were a lot of kind of overwrought comparisons I think in the last few days. But man, this was a big moment to have an American president next to the Russian president saying these things.",
